Who killed a four-month-sized son in a state of intoxicated novocherboksary will appear before the court
A resident of Chuvashia will be in the dock for the murder of her own child.
In Cheboksary completed the investigation of the criminal case against a 31-year-old resident of Novocheboksarsk, accused of causing death by negligence.
The investigation established that on July 26 of this year the accused, together with a four-month-old son, came to celebrate the child’s birthday in Cheboksary.
After a long feast, the novocheboksara decided to stay away and put the child to go to sleep.
When the baby woke up and crippled at night, a drunk woman put it next to her on the bed to breastfeed, but soon fell asleep.
During the time of sleep, the woman piled on her son, as a result of which he died of suffocation.
The criminal case, according to the press-provided service of the Investigative TFR in Chuvashia, was sent to the court.
Inflation came out of a vacation.
will the Russians feel a sharp increase in prices
As a result of cross-sanctions of the Russian Federation and the West, inflation in Russia will accelerate and will not meet the forecasts of the authorities, VTB Capital analysts are convinced.
According to experts, the delayed consequences of anti-sanctions will lead to an inflationary shock in the first quarter of 2015, writes RBC Daily.
According to the latest forecast of the Ministry of economic development, in 2014 inflation will be 6.5-7.5%, next year and even less - 6.5%.
However, according to the Kommersant newspaper, this week the MAYOR should make “small but principle-pronucinative” adjustments to the macroeconomic forecast until 2017.
According to the publication of the publication, the Agency may reduce the assessment of inflation for 2015, which will allow to eliminate the expectant zeroing of population income growth.
In the forecast it was assumed that the food sanctions of the Russian Federation are in force for a year - until August 2015.
How in such a situation it was possible to assume such high inflagration?
The current situation implies a one-time shock, but the downward trend of inflation will not change, ”said the source of the newspaper in the Ministry of Finance.
Economists from "VTB Capital" do not share the expectations of the financial and economic block of the government regarding inflation and predict a rise in prices in 2014 at 8%.
The main effect of food bans will come along with the possible introduction of a sales tax in the regions of the Russian Federation in 2015, experts are convinced.
An inflationary peak of 9% will occur in February, after which the annual growth rates of prices will decline, experts believe.
By the way, last week Rosstat summed up the first inflationary results after Russia imposed “neopal sanctions”.
AiFT.ru interviewed the experts and found out their inflation expectations.
Artem Deev, Head of the Analytical FC AForex:
The increase in prices is already observed according to the main segments of the goods.
Moreover, when socially significant products began to rise in price, the government was forced to launch measures of systematic monitoring of price stability in order to prevent the risks of an unreasonable increase in prices in the short term.
Venerienters and retailers are already reporting on a daily basis to regulators so that the authorities know exactly who, when and for what reason it initiates a price hike.
Colossal rates of capital outflow, excessive refinancing of the banking sector from the Russian mega-regulator, internal conversion due to the flight of the population into a foreign currency, as well as an embargo on the import of food products from countries that have acceded to sanctions against Russia continue to be the key factors in the growth of the consumer price.
If infliliary pressure remained under scrutiny, the cost of a basket of a minimum set of products wouldn’t have jumped by 12 per cent in just one quarter.
The ban on the import of food products provoked a supply shock in the domestic market and led to an absolutely logical increase in the final cost.
Since at the current stage we are not able to fully start the mechanism of import substitution, as well as to increase self-sufficiency of the Russian market due to domestic production, retaliatory sanctions will continue to income the risks of an increase in the inflationary component.
If we also add here the fiscal reasons associated with the lack of confidence in the national currency, as well as impaired prospects for economic growth, by the end of this year, inflation may well exceed 8%.
Aleksandr Razuwaev, Director of the Analytical Department "Alpari"
In the 90-ies, inflation sometimes reached 20-30% per a month, it was also higher than the current zero level, it’s another quench that at that time it was compensated by rapid economic growth.
I think at the end of the year, inflation will amount to 8-8.5%.
Platon Maguta, asset manager of the Magut Foundation
The external factor associated with the sanctions against Russia is still successfully restrained, but if there is a further increase in pressure, first of all, on the financial sector - this can lead to certain difficulties and an increase the inflationary expectations of the population, which are usually traditionally manifested in the purchases of cash currency in order to protect their savings.
We can assume that the government, the Central Bank of the Russian Federation and the Ministry of Finance will try to soften the smooth easing of the ruble, both market methods and state regulation.
But the increase in prices traditionally begins after a summer lull and, perhaps, this autumn will become more noticeable.
The target level of consumer inflation in Russia at 7.5%, most likely, will withstand, since the Russian Federation still has financial savings.
Horticultural centers grieve from a decrease in the number of landlords
Such a drop, due to an exceptional decrease in the number of homeowners under the age of less than the age of 35, can cause horticultural sanctuaries to begin to lose tens of millions of pounds a year, when today's young consumers reach the “basomial age of horticulture”, according to a HTA study reported by the Financial Times.
According to this report, people who take housing spend an average of 55 per cent of the amount that owners of their own homes spend on horticulture.
It mentions an increasing number of people living in highly hurbanized ordains, the popularity of asphalting yards in front of a parking house and a decrease in the size of the garden as other factors that pose a threat to an industry whose annual sales are estimated to be £5-bill.
In Greater-London, where the number of homeowners has fallen from 61 per cent to 43 per cent in six years, the UK has the lowest level of spending for gardeners per household.
HTA and the Royal horticultural society reported that renting a dwelling and lack of garden space does not mean that people can’t plunder plants.
Guy Bartter, chief horticulture adviser at RHS, said: “Growing plants in containers, for example, it’s especially popular for tenants who can transport plants with them when they move.”
The HTA report speculated the period between 1997 and 2005 as the “golden age” of horticulture retail trade as a result of an increase in the number of homeowners and economic predominance from the late 1980’s until the mid-1990s.
It also predicts that the market will improve this year thanks to better weather after unfavourable conditions in March and April of last year.
