Kynia registers civil servants to Identify “invisibility workers”
Kenya began biometric registration of all civil servants in an attempt to remove “invisibility workers” from the government’s payroll.
Employees who do not register for the next two weeks will no longer be paid, according to a government statement.
The government suspects that thousands of people continue to receive salaries after leaving public service.
President Uhuru Keniyat has pledged to cut corruption in state structures after coming to power in 2013.
An auditor’s audit earlier this year found that at least $1 million (£70000) per month disappears due to disbursement of “invisibility workers” and other financial irregularities.
The government suspects that salaries continue to go into bank accounts, even after a person dies or leaves public service, BBC reporter Vanyama Chebusiri from the capital, Nairobi.
All civil servants must appear over the next two weeks at identification centers to ensure their data are collected through a biometric registration procedure, according to a government statement.
Anyone who doesn’t do this without a good reason will be erased from a payroll.
“This procedure will materially contribute to the rationalization of the civil service, determining the actual number of civil servants, and will also be used to clear the payroll at both levels of the government – thus puts an end to “invisible workers,” said Anne Vaigru, Secretary of the Cabinet of Ministers of Planning and structuring.
