Lesotho's Army Says Coincidence Wasn't Exploded; Prime Minister Remains in South Africa
Lesotho's army officials refuted the fact of preparing a coup to overthrow the government, stating that they acted against the police, who were suspected of attempting to arm political fanatics.
Prime Minister Thomas Tabánate left the country, stating that the country's army had enclosed its official residence and seized government buildings in Maseru.
The Prime Minister took his family to neighboring South Africa, stating that he was threatened with an attack on his life.
The representative of the army, Major Nételle Ntoi, said that there was, in fact, a coffin, but that the army reacted to the threat from the “political fanatics” whom the police tried to arm.
“What Happened This Morning Is That Lesothy’s Armed Forces took action, having received several intelligence reports that there are certain elements among the police that really planned to arm some political, young fanatics of political parties who were ready to cause chaos,” he told the Voice of America.
The representative of the South-African government Clyzón Mongiella said that the actions of the army looked like a coup.
“Although no one has filed claims to seize power by force, according to all accounts, the actions of the Lesotho armed forces at the moment have the main signs of the coup,” he said.
Lesotho army officials said that the soldiers returned to the barracks on Sunday and that in the capital is calm.
In the meantime, deputy prime minister of Moetzhou Metsing runs the government in the absence of Tabán.
Tabáne said that he believes that he is being attacked in connection with an attempt to fight corruption in the country.
The situation in Lesotho is very tense since June, when Tabán suspended parliamentary sessions as a result of disagreements in its unified government.
He said that his actions did not jeopardize the government, regardless of accusations to the contrary.
