Proceedings in the euro zone at the lowest in 13 months
The growth of production in the euro zone slowed to the lowest figure in 13 months in August, according to a popular study.
The last value of the business activism index (PMI) of the industrial sector by Markit fell to 50.7 in August, dropping from 51.8 in July.
The value of more than 50 indicates an increase.
The new orders were delayed, and enterprises suffered among the growing tension between the EU and Russia in connection with Ukraine.
Measurements were received in front of the meeting of the European Central Bank (ECB) on Thursday.
The markets will be expecting the bank a clear plan on how to cope with stopping the recovery of the eurozone, as well as the threat of deflation with an influenza of only 0.3%.
There is an assumption that the head of the ECB Mario Dragi later this week can give additional signals that he is considering a quantitative easing scheme for the eurozone, similar to those used by the United Kingdom and the United States during the financial crisis.
“Although any increase is better than no increase at all, the bragging effect of increasing economic and geopolitical uncertainty on manufacturers is becoming more apparent,” said Rob Dobson, senior economist at Markit.
PMI of the production sector of Germany, Russia's largest trading partner in the European Union, fell to the lowest level in 11 months.
In the meantime, in the second-largest economy of the block, France, PMI fell to 46.
France continues to arouse real concern, as well as the weakening of Italy from stable growth to stagnation.
There’s signs that the growth boost has faded mostly the industrial engine, Germany, as well as in the Spain and the Netherlands, doesn’t add to the certainty,” said Mr Dobson.
Slaughter of the industry, most likely, will continue to add fuel to analysts who are awaiting the introduction of additional monetary or fiscal stimulus stimulus.
One positive news came from Ireland where PMI has risen to $57.3, its highest level since the end of 1999.
Howard Archer, the chief economist of IHS Global Insight, said: “The best we can say about an August study of the economic activity index of the eurozone’s industrial sector business, that it shows that the sector is still growing.”
He added: “Eurozone Employers obviously find life very difficult at the present moment, since the current increased geopolitical tension – especially Russia/Ukraina-affiliated – adds to uncertainties in many countries.
This increased tensions evidently have hit the business – and especially consumer confidence, and is likely to cause some orders lingerered or even abrogated, especially large-custodous orders.
He said that “more and more like the fact that the ECB, of course, will have to carry out some kind of quantitative easing”, “although we suspect that it will be limited.”
