The Chinese Parliament decided on Sunday not to allow Hong Kong voters to nominate candidates for the elections of 2017, despite the growing demands of democratic reform.
Such a move is most likely to give rise to long-awaited protests in Hong Kong's business district, since activists began planning and mobilizing in a matter of hours after this statement.
The decision of the All-Chinese assembly of popular representatives, in fact, allows the communist leaders to remove all candidates who are not loyal to Beijing.
“It didn’t defraud our expectations, but still infuriates us,” said lawmaker Emily Lau, chairman of the Democratic Party.
It’s not what Beijing promised.
Deceiving Hong Kong people.
And it is obvious that we are dealing with an authoritarian regime.
Defending the decision of China, Li Fey, deputy secretary-general of the Standing Committee of the All-China People's Congress, said it would be too “choote” to allow the popular nomination of the Hong Kong leader.
Since 1997, when the U.G. transferred control of Hong Kong back to China, Beijing has pledged to allow the inhabitants of the region to choose the head of the government, starting in 2017.
The Chinese leaders presented Sunday’s decision as a democratic step forward, as it enjoining Hong Kong people to a direct vote; however, the decision also makes it obvious that Chinese leaders will continue to hold the process in their own hands with the basis of a commission to nominate candidates tightly controlled from Beijing.
And, according to the new article, only the candidates who “love the country and love Hong Kong” will be allowed.
The decision was made after the summer, when there were some of the most large-propagated protests in Hong Kong for many years.
Behind a large part of the pro-democracy campaign in Hong Kong is the Occupy Central With Love and Peace movement, whose organisers have threatened to oversteer Hong Kong's financial district if Beijing does not guarantee real universal suffrage.
On Sunday in the evening, already a few hours after the statement, hundreds of Occupy Central adherents gathered in the rain in front of the headquarters of the government of Hong Kong.
At the demonstrators said that their movement is moving to a new stage of civil disobedience and that they will rise on the waves of protest in the coming weeks.
Nevertheless, they didn't provide any details, apparently seeking to avoid problems with state bodies.
In an online statement, the organizers reported that the movement “considered the possibility of capturing the central region only as an exceptional measure, an action that can only be taken if all the possibilities of the dialog are exhausted and there is no other choice.
It is regrettable to informing us that to date all the possibilities of the dialogue have been exhausted and the seizure of the central region will undoubtedly take place.
The state authorities in Hong Kong were preparing for Beijing for several days, and security measures were reinforced at the head of the government on Sunday with the involvement of police and barricades.
At the heart of turmoil lies the feeling of many in Hong Kong that they are slowly losing control of their own city.
The influx of inhabitants from the mainland increases competition in the field of goods and services.
It also heightens the Fleecie that Hong Kong’s values, such as democracies and freedom of speech, begin to weaken under increasing pressure from Beijing.
Some criticize the Occupy Central movement, saying that his demonstrations are at risk for Hong Kong.
“The protest they are talking about can lead to a lot of economic detriment, depending on how many people will participate and how long”, said the legislator Regina Ip, who has long criticized the movement.
We don’t want to worry that Hong Kong spirals out of control.
This is an impression that is bad for investment.
Chinese state-renstrikes have also been reporting in recent days in which Hong Kong activists of the democratic movement have been shown by saboteurs sent by Western powers.
This summer the activists organized an unofficial referendum on the electoral law, which attracted 78000 participants - more than one-one-fifth of Hong Kong voters.
And in July, tens of thousands went to one of the largest pro-democracy demonstrations in the history of the region.
