Car Recycling Program Rescrepancreas Reschedule Reschedule
From September 1, the program for the disposal of cars has been reopened in Russia.
Until December 31, 2014, 10 billion rubles will be allocated for these purposes.
Denis Mantruov, head of the Ministry of Industry and Trade of the Russian Federation explained, the money is allocated from the limits, which have already been communicated to the Ministry of Industry and Commerce of the Russian Federation through redistribution from other areas of support.
The program will affect all cars older than six years.
The amount of compensation will be from 40 thousand for a passenger car up to 350 thousand rubles for a truck.
Earlier in Russia there has already been a similar car recycling program.
The main difference of the current project, the Minister explained, is that now it is supposed not only the recycling of cars to obtain a subsequent discount, but also an opportunity, if the car has reached the age of more than six years, to purchase a new with a dispensation when handing the old one to the “trade-in” from the dealer, Interfax reports.
More than 16 billion rubles have been spent on the previous federal recycling program, which was in effect from January 1, 2010 to December 31, 2011.
Thanks to it, the level of production of passenger cars in 2010 amounted to about 1.2 million units (this plus 101.4% in relation to 2009).
The creator of Coming Out Simulator will publish a game about modern journalism
Game designer Nikki Cayce, well-renowned from Coming Out Simulator decided to release a game about modern journalism.
He spoke about his project in an Interview with the Kill Screen.
Cayce began work on the game a few weeks ago, but this fact is widely known only now.
The source of the inspiration for the game was the events in Ferguson, where there are now protests, which began after a policeman killed an unarmed black teenager.
The design of the project, which does not yet have a name, will be how the information coverage of stories affects their consequences.
The herator of the game, a civilian journalist, will need to take pictures of what is happening in areas where clashes take place between the police and the population.
The hero can take a different picture of what is happening, which will affect the course of events.
If he removes the scene in such a way that the cops will look like heroes, and the protesters - bandits, the police will begin to treat him better to treat him and go where an ordinary person can not pass.
If it covers the events differently, his photographs could turn peaceful protests into widespread riots.
Glasses in the game will need to be dialed with the help of a hero tweet, in which new subscribers will appear, if the photos laid out there will turn out to be popular.
The game doesn't have a name yet.
The release date and platforms on which it will appear is unknown.
The incident, however, published an image that demonstrates how an event can change, depending on how a photo is taken and processed about it.
In an interview with the Kill Screen, Cayce explained that he was pondering the concept of such a game for quite a long time a long time, but originally wanted to dedicate it not to protests in the United States, but to events in the Gaza Strip.
After he began to collect the material for the game, the game designer realized that he lacked the knowledge and skills to illuminate the history of the region.
However, when he saw how the media covers the events in Ferguson, he decided to make a game based on what happened.
Nikki Cayce is an independent developer who releasing browser games.
Its most well-knowledged project was Coming Out Simulator, which came out in 2014.
This game, which tells about events from the biography of Cayce himself, is devoted to how a bisexual teenager is trying to tell homophobic parents about her relationship.
