Prison officials of Bulgaria held a national protest
Hundreds of prison employees from all over Bulgaria held a national protest before the Ministry of justice in the capital of Sophia.
During the peaceful demonstration, they reiterated their demands to return the previous working conditions that have recently been alternated.
The increase in salaries is also included in the list of requirements, which will be negotiated with the acting Minister of Justice Hristo Ivanov.
During the month, the officers expressed a symbolic protest against a new schedule of work, demanding to restore the previous 24-hour shifts.
Despite the fact that the union of prison employees repeatedly met with representatives of the Ministry of justice, an agreement was not reached, according to the Bolgar national radio (BPR).
Negotiations are continuing, the head of the Main Directorate for the Execution of Sentences Rossen Zhylyaskov said.
It is expected that members of the Federation of Commerce of Employees of the Ministry of Internal Affairs are expected to contribute to a protest of the prison employees’ union.
The army controls the new government of Thailand
General Prayuta (right) appointed to the post of Minister of Defense of Pravita Wongsuvan (left)
The military ruler and prime minister of Thailand, General Prayut Chang Ocha, announced the composition of the country’s new cabinet, in which more than one-a-three-departing office is held by generals or retired generals.
Representatives of the army received key positions: ministers of defense, finance, foreign affairs and trade.
On Monday, Prayut Chang Ocha was officially confirmed as prime minister after the king of Thailand officially approved the appointment of the head of an interim martial government to this post.
60-year-old soldier, who seized power in the country as a result of a bloodless coup on May 22, was the only candidate for the premier post.
He was Elected as prime minister at a parliamentary meeting.
Earlier, the army appointed members of the Parliament.
According to Prayuta Chan-Ocha, the revolution was the only way to maintain stability.
He was preceded by six-seven-year demonstrations, during which the protesters demanded the resignation of Prime Minister Yingglak Shinawatra.
Officially, Prayut Chang Ocha will be a temporary prime minister, since the military plans to hold general elections at the end of 2015.
However, there are fears that the military are trying to consolidate their power in the country.
the military came to power on May 22
against the backdrop of complete paralysis of civil power.
The leaders of the main political parties were temporarily arrested.
Immediately after the military came to power, they provisionally arrested the country's leading politicians, including Prime Minister Yinglyk Shinawatra.
Some journalists and public figures were also arrested.
The Current Political Crim-Eating Crisque into Removing Brother Yinglyk Chinawat Taksina as prime minister.
Both of them are very popular among the rural population, especially in the northern regions of the country, which has repeatedly ensured their success in the elections.
However, many representatives of the middle class and the capital elite entered the anti-government movement, which since November 2013 has practically paralyzed the country.
