Deputies proposed to exacerbate the entry into force of the law on personal data
In the case of the success of the initiative, these Russians can not be stored on servers abroad from January 1, 2015
The state Duma introduced a draft law that provides for the postponement of the date of entry into force of the already adopted law on personal data of Russians from September 1, 2016 to January 1, 2015.
The initiative is positioned at the base of legislative activities of the lower house.
Its authors were deputies of the state Duma Vadim Dengin, Yaroslav Nilov (LDPR), Evgeny Fedorov (“United Russia”), Vladimir Parakhin (“Fair Russia”) and Alexander Yushchenko (CPRF).
Such a change, according to the authors of the initiative, will contribute more quickly and effective to ensure the rights of citizens of the Russian Federation to the preservation of personal data and the secrecy of correspondence in information and telecommunication networks.
The law, the entry into force of which parliamentarians is in a hurry obliges to store personal data of Russians processed via the Internet on the territory of the Russian Federation.
It also makes mandatory the registration, systematization, accumulation, storage, refinement and retrieval of personal data of citizens of the Russian Federation, in the databases located on the territory of Russia, as well as an indication of information about the location of such databases.
In addition, the law gives Roskomnadzor the right on the basis of a court’s decision to restrict access to information processed in violation of the law of the Russian Federation in the field of personal data.
The law also proposes the creation of the so-called “black lists” of sites-inviolators - an automated information system "Register of violators of the rights of personal data subjects".
This law was adopted by the state Duma in the summer.
The authors of the bill were Andrey Lugovoi and Vadim Dengin from LDPR and Alexander Yushchenko from the CPRF.
Dengin and Yushchenko are also one of the authors of the current bill to postpone the date of entry into force of an already adopted law.
To those who do not have data centers in the Russian Federation will have to pay for the rent
Commenting on the bill submitted to the state Duma today by the first Deputy head of the Duma Committee on information policy Leonid Levin said that he does not see the problems to postpone the date of entry into force of the law on the protection of personal data.
According to him, those companies that by January 1, 2015 will not have their own repositories of personal data of Russians on the territory of the Russian Federation, will be able to rent them, for example, from Rostelecom, which has repeatedly said that they are ready to provide their servers for rent and already provides their servers, from Mail Group, from Western companies that are already providing their servers today.
“In addition, there are several independent data centers who are ready to cooperate in this area”, – he added.
“If the companies decide to comply with the law and continue to work on the territory of the Russian Federation, I don’t see the problems – they will receive technological opportunities for placement,” Levin summed up.
Controversial Bill
Immediately after the publication of the draft of the draft law, the Russian Association of electronic communications expressed concern that in the event of the adoption of the bill the Russians will no longer be able to use many global Internet services.
In particular, we are talking about the services of online booking of air tickets and hotels.
In the opinion of the association, “the bill deprives the subjects of personal data - citizens of the Russian Federation the right to dispose of their own data imperatively determining that their recording, systematization, etc. should be ensured only in databases located on the territory of the Russian Federation.
In a statement, RAEC emphasizes that a more effective measure would be to create a mechanism for informing the user about where the registration is carried out, systematizing his personal data.
In such a case, he himself will be able to choose in what territory the specified actions will be carried out.
The adoption of such bills on the “localization” of personal data in other countries led to the withdrawal of global services and to significant economic losses.
