The Norwegian Cruise Approaches $3-billion-dollar Deal with Prestige Cruises
The Norwegian Cruise Line Holdings Ltd NCHL.O, the third-largest cruise operator in the world, is at the final stage of negotiations to acquire a similar company Prestige Cruises International Inc for about $ 3 billion, according to people familiar with the situation.
The deal would provide Norwegian Cruise, a $6.8-billion-pronminated company with a $6.8-billion-vehicle-vehicle-vehicle-vehicle-vehicle-vehicles, as it competes with larger rivals Royal Caribbean Cruises Ltd (RCL.N) and Carnival Corp (FCL.N).
The agreement could be announced as early as this week, sources said on Sunday, warning that the negotiations could still fail.
Prestige Cruises, private investment company Apollo Global Management LLC (APO.N), also owns 20 per cent stake in Norwegian Cruise.
The sources asked to remain unnamed, since the negotiations are not open.
Representatives of the Norwegian Cruise and Prestige Cruises did not respond to requests to give comments, while the Apollo representative refused to comment on the information.
Founded in Miami, based in Miami, manages 13 cruise ships on routes spanning North America, the Mediterranean, the Baltic Sea, Central America and the Caribbean.
Its income amounted to $ 2.57 billion in 2013, 13 percent more than in 2012.
Prestige Cruises, also based in Miami, works under Oceania and Regent, which together owns eight cruise ships that go to Scandinavia, Russia, the Mediterranean, North America, Asia, Africa, and South America.
She announced an income of $ 1.2 billion in 2013, 6 percent higher than a year earlier.
It is expected that the $29-billion-dollar cruise industry is expected to be positively reflected in the growth of the middle class in developing economies, such as China and India.
Race companies strive to position themselves as cruise operators, preferred by these new customers.
Prestige Cruises was registered with U.S. Regulatory Authorities for primary public offering in January 2014.
Apollo became the company's main shareholder after an $850-million-dollar deal.
Norwegian Cruise was created in the form as it exists now, in 2000 as a result of the merger with a cruise operator belonging to Genting Bhd (GENT.KL), an entertainment conglomerate and a casino controlled by the Malasian billionaire Lim Cock Tey.
Apollo invested $1 billion in Norwegian Cruise in 2008.
The Norwegian Cruise turned into a public company in January 2013.
Genting owned a share of 28 per cent, Apollo owned 20 per cent, and private equity firm TGP Capital LP owned an 8% stake of 8 per cent, according to data at the end of June, according to statements.
Carnival, The Royal Caribbean Cruises and the Norwegian Cruise together account for 82 percent of the cruise passenger traffic in North America, according to registration documents at the first public offering of Prestige Cruises shares.
