Putin demanded from Kiev to start negotiations on the “statehood” of Eastern Ukraine
According to Russian news reports about his remarks, Russian President Vladimir Putin on Sunday demanded that the Ukrainian government stop fighting with the separatists in the east of the country and immediately began negotiations about the “statehood” of the detached region.
Its press secretary, Dmitry Peskov, later clarified that Putin did not mean hinting that the Eastern-Ukrainian territory will become part of the separatist-controlled, but that its status within Ukraine needs to revise in order to give the Russian-speaking region an opportunity to protect their rights and interests.
However, Putin’s appeal to the Kiev government to begin negotiations with pro-Russian rebels as equally corresponded to an obvious strategy, which he follows from the beginning of the bloodshed five months ago: to help the separatists to take the territory and force the Ukrainian government to give the newly proclamated territory of Novorossia de facto independence in order to converge with Russia, rather than the West.
