The TsT terminated the investigation of the Tu-204 disaster in Vnukovo
The Investigative Committee of Russia (TFR) has terminated the criminal case on the fact of the crash of the Tu-204 airline Red Wings.
This is reported by the press service of the department.
The decision was made in connection with the death of the commander of the aircraft and a flight engineer.
The forensic examination showed that the cause of the disaster was an increased approach rate, non-performance by the crew of standard landing procedures, as well as unintentional output of the engines for increased direct thrust.
The Tu-204 of Red Wyings, en route the Paga-Moscow flight, crashed on December 29, 2012 while landing at the Vnukovo airport in the Moscow Vnukovo airport.
The aircraft rolled out the runway and caught fire.
At the scene of the incident were the commander of the aircraft, a co-pilot and a flight engineer.
two flight attendants were taken to medical institutions, where they subsequently died from injuries.
Another three flight attendants, hospitalized with grievous bodily bodily injuries, were undergoing a long-term treatment.
In addition, grievous bodily injury was the driver of the car, moving at the time of the disaster along the Kiev highway.
There were no passengers on board an aircraft.
The traffic prosecutor's office after the disaster conducted an inspection in Red Wings, during which numerous violations were revealed.
In February 2013, the airline's license was suspended.
The company resumed work in the summer of 2013.
